  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Two billion years ago, Earth built its own nuclear reactor. In 1972, French scientists testing uranium ore from Gabon found that the fuel had already been burned.",
        "visual_query": "gabon equatorial african rock formations",
        "visual_fallback": "geological rock layers"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Key isotopes of uranium-235 were missing, leaving behind the exact chemical byproduct found in modern nuclear waste. It looked like an ancient power plant.",
        "visual_query": "uranium ore mineral deposit macro",
        "visual_fallback": "glowing crystal minerals"
      },
      {
        "narration": "How could atomic fuel be consumed before humans even existed? Geologists began analyzing the surrounding rock.",
        "visual_query": "open pit mining excavation rock",
        "visual_fallback": "deep rock canyon"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They discovered an impossible coincidence. Two billion years ago, natural uranium was far more concentrated, and seeping groundwater naturally slowed neutrons down, igniting spontaneous fission.",
        "visual_query": "water seeping through sandstone bedrock",
        "visual_fallback": "underground cave stream"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Whenever it boiled, steam escaped and shut the reaction off until it cooled, running a self-regulating nuclear reactor for hundreds of thousands of years.",
        "visual_query": "natural hot steam rising rocks",
        "visual_fallback": "steam rising landscape"
      }
    ],
